<w:document xmlns:w="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main" xmlns:m="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/math" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:v="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:vml" xmlns:w10="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:pic="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/picture" xmlns:wp="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/wordprocessingDrawing" xmlns:w14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/word/2010/wordml" xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006" mc:Ignorable="w14"><w:body><w:p w14:paraId="7DB1625A"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">User:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="59327232"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Is there any package that can implement the Gutzwiller projection on the fermionic Gaussian state, and compute the overlap between two projected states?</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7E1F2DDE"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="4F2A2422"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Kimi:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="63874AB0"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Yes — the closest match is </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">TeMFpy</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">, and depending on your setting (1D vs. higher dimensions, exact vs. Monte Carlo), here are the realistic options:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkStart w:id="20" w:name="temfpy-python-1d-the-most-direct-answer"/><w:p w14:paraId="36740CAA"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">1. TeMFpy (Python, 1D — the most direct answer)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7189051E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">TeMFpy (Hille &amp; Szabó, 2025) is a library built on TeNPy that converts fermionic mean-field states — both Slater determinants and Pfaffian (Bogoliubov/BCS-type Gaussian) states — into finite or infinite MPS form, and it explicitly includes a Gutzwiller projection facility (</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:rStyle w:val="VerbatimChar"/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">temfpy.gutzwiller</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">, e.g. </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:rStyle w:val="VerbatimChar"/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">abrikosov_ph()</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> for Abrikosov-fermion spin-liquid constructions). Once both projected states are MPS, the overlap ⟨Ψ₁|Ψ₂⟩ is just a standard exact MPS contraction — so this package covers both halves of your question. It’s on PyPI (</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:rStyle w:val="VerbatimChar"/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">pip install temfpy</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">) and MIT-licensed.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="622A86FE"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Caveat: MPS is inherently a 1D (or quasi-1D/cylinder) representation, so this is the right tool for chains and ladders, not 2D thermodynamic lattices.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="20"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="21" w:name="mvmc-c-any-dimension-monte-carlo"/><w:p w14:paraId="60E8944E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">2. mVMC (C, any dimension — Monte Carlo)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="1E6B5BB4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">If you want Gutzwiller-projected BCS/Pfaffian wave functions in 2D or 3D, </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">mVMC</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> (Misawa et al.) natively implements the fully projected (g→∞) Gutzwiller wave function and evaluates its amplitudes via Pfaffians. It also explicitly supports computing overlaps between two </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:i/><w:iCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">different</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> optimized variational wave functions (the standard tool for excited-state/nodal-surface calculations). This gives you the overlap, but as a statistical Monte Carlo estimate rather than an exact number.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="21"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="22" w:name="Xb3d65efc1b503ad4a77d36f8be46f02b3ee1e3b"/><w:p w14:paraId="757FA2A8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">3. Research codes (not packaged, but adaptable)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7DC2AC3E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1001"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">QSpace-based codes</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> for the U(1)-GfPEPS construction of Li, van Delft &amp; Tu, where Gutzwiller projection is applied as a product of local operators and projected Dirac spin-liquid states are contracted with CTMRG — this is a PEPS (2D) route, but the code is research-grade, not a library.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="792B9FAE"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1001"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">The </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">MPO–MPS method</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> (Wu et al.; Liu, Tu et al.) converts Gutzwiller-projected paired fermion states to MPS by applying Bogoliubov-quasiparticle MPOs to the vacuum; projection is a D=1 MPO applied afterward. These are published algorithms rather than maintained packages, but TeMFpy effectively packages and improves this line of work.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="22"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="23" w:name="one-theoretical-caveat-worth-knowing"/><w:p w14:paraId="6ECD1332"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">One theoretical caveat worth knowing</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="3C1538EE"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">For </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">unprojected</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> Gaussian states, the overlap is a single Pfaffian/determinant (Balian–Brézin / Onishi formula), which TeMFpy uses internally for its fast overlap routines. For </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">projected</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> states there is no equally universal closed form — the overlap becomes a sum over particle-number/spin sectors, which is exactly why people either go through MPS (exact, 1D) or Monte Carlo (any dimension). In special 1D cases (Jordan–Wigner-solvable paired states), recent work does derive exact Pfaffian formulas for projected-state overlaps.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="42015744"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Bottom line:</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> if your system is 1D, use TeMFpy; if it’s 2D/3D and you can live with statistical error, use mVMC. If you tell me the geometry and whether you need exact or sampled overlaps, I can sketch the concrete workflow.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="521B225A"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="34BFA436"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">User:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="47622E94"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Suppose I want to do this calculation in two dimensions with periodic boundary condition. What is the best way to do this computation for small system sizes, e.g., 4*4 unit cells?</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="335154FA"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="20ED1BE4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Kimi:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6F5A6570"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">For a 4×4 cluster (16 sites) with periodic boundaries, the right tool is </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">exact enumeration in Fock space using Pfaffian/determinant amplitudes</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — not Monte Carlo and not MPS. Here’s why and how.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="23"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="24" w:name="Xb90d2276ebb85385a78cfd2c56288aad55d40f1"/><w:p w14:paraId="706839B6"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Why enumeration is the right method at this size</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5979E3A2"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">The Gutzwiller projector P_G = ∏</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:i/><w:iCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">i (1 − n</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">{i↑}n_{i↓}) annihilates all doubly occupied configurations. For a half-filled state (16 electrons on 16 sites), the projected wave function has support </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">only on singly-occupied configurations</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — i.e., classical spin configurations. That subspace has dimension</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="501EE450"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1002"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">2¹⁶ = 65,536 (singlet/singly-occupied sector),</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7D88F740"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1002"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">or 3¹⁶ ≈ 4.3×10⁷ if your BCS state fluctuates in particle number and you need empty sites too.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="1926379A"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Both are trivially enumerable. No Monte Carlo error, no sign problem, no bond-dimension convergence — you get the exact overlap as a finite sum.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="24"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="25" w:name="the-algorithm"/><w:p w14:paraId="49B1146C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">The algorithm</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7F41F470"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Setup.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> Represent each Gaussian state by its matrix: - Slater determinant: the N_σ × N_s coefficient matrix U (per spin, or 2N×2N block for paired orbitals); - General paired (BCS) Gaussian state: the antisymmetric 2N_s × 2N_s matrix Z in |Ψ⟩ = exp(½ Σ Z_ij c_i† c_j†)|0⟩.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="70193264"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Amplitudes.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> For any configuration c (a set of occupied modes), the unprojected amplitude is a minor: - Slater: ⟨c|Ψ⟩ = det U_c (submatrix of occupied rows) - BCS: ⟨c|Ψ⟩ = Pf(Z_cc) (2n×2n submatrix; n = number of pairs)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6E7E151C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t>Both are O(n³) per configuration — this is the standard fermionic-Gaussian “fidelity” trick, and it’s exactly the machinery TeMFpy uses internally.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="57BBB53A"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Projection + overlap.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> The projected overlap is just a weighted sum over the surviving configurations:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="3A3F4DD2"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><m:oMathPara><m:oMathParaPr><m:jc m:val="center"/></m:oMathParaPr><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟨</m:t></m:r><m:sSubSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Ψ</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>P</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>1</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSubSup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="|"/><m:endChr m:val="|"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:sSubSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Ψ</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>P</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>2</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSubSup><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟩</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>=</m:t></m:r><m:nary><m:naryPr><m:chr m:val="∑"/><m:limLoc m:val="undOvr"/><m:subHide m:val="off"/><m:supHide m:val="on"/></m:naryPr><m:sub><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>:</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>no double occupancy</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:r><m:t>​</m:t></m:r></m:sup><m:e><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟨</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:nary><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Ψ</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>1</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSup></m:e></m:d><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟩</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟨</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>|</m:t></m:r><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Ψ</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>2</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSup><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>⟩</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>=</m:t></m:r><m:nary><m:naryPr><m:chr m:val="∑"/><m:limLoc m:val="undOvr"/><m:subHide m:val="off"/><m:supHide m:val="on"/></m:naryPr><m:sub><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:r><m:t>​</m:t></m:r></m:sup><m:e><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>P</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>f</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:nary><m:sSup><m:e><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:sSubSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Z</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>1</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSubSup></m:e></m:d></m:e><m:sup><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>*</m:t></m:r></m:sup></m:sSup><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>P</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>f</m:t></m:r><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:sSubSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>Z</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:t>c</m:t></m:r></m:sub><m:sup><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:r><m:t>2</m:t></m:r></m:e></m:d></m:sup></m:sSubSup></m:e></m:d></m:oMath></m:oMathPara></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6FC74670"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Since P_G is diagonal in the occupation basis, it simply restricts the sum. Normalize each state by its own projected norm ⟨Ψ_P|Ψ_P⟩ = Σ_c |Pf(Z_cc)|², which you compute the same way.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="4D5B9B50"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Cost.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> 65,536 configurations × ~16³ per Pfaffian ≈ 10⁹ flops per state-pair — seconds on a laptop. Even 5×5 (2²⁵ = 3×10⁷ configs) is parallelizable to a minute or so.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="25"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="26" w:name="practical-notes"/><w:p w14:paraId="48F53258"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Practical notes</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="36F1CBAA"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1003"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">PBC changes nothing</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> in this approach — the momentum structure only enters through how you build Z (or U); the enumeration is over real-space occupation patterns, which are basis-independent.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="2B21C4E4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1003"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Particle number sectors.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> If your two states live in different (N_↑, N_↓) sectors, the overlap is exactly zero — the sum enforces this automatically since no configuration appears in both. For number-fluctuating BCS states, just sum over all compatible sectors (each restricted to no-double-occupancy).</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="38B3D8F2"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1003"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Spinful vs. spinless.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> For spinless fermions (relevant for some spin-liquid constructions, e.g. Abrikosov-fermion PEPS), the projected state is supported on configs with at most one fermion per site; the counting is even simpler.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7C9CC556"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1003"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Sanity checks:</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> (1) your projected norm should reproduce ⟨Ψ|P_G|Ψ⟩ which you can also compute via covariance-matrix Wick contractions as a cross-check; (2) for two identical states the overlap formula must return the norm squared.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="26"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="27" w:name="when-youd-pick-something-else"/><w:p w14:paraId="3D29A020"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">When you’d pick something else</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="4E9DC9C0"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1004"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">mVMC</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">: works, and for a 16-site cluster it converges beautifully with ~10⁸ samples — but it’s a stochastic answer to a problem that has an exact one. Use it only as a cross-check.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7300201C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1004"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">TeMFpy/MPS</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">: not recommended here. MPS is a 1D representation; on a 4×4 PBC cluster mapped to a snake you’d get long-range bonds and an unnecessarily large effective bond dimension. The direct Pfaffian sum is both simpler and exact.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6CCC4D74"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">If you tell me the precise form of your states (spinful/spinless, number-conserving Slater vs. paired BCS, and how the two states differ — e.g., different pairing matrices), I can write you a compact, ready-to-run Python script that does the enumeration and returns the normalized overlap.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="11D58A1E"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5CA479BE"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">User:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="43E696F8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">How does the computation complexity scales in the exact enumeration method, given fixed number of unit cells but varying number of orbitals within each unit cell? For 4*4 unit cells, what is a reasonable and computable number of orbitals?</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="43E8CD64"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6E209730"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Kimi:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="61FBF2DC"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Good question — the scaling has a clean structure, and the key point is that the </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:i/><w:iCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">expensive part</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> and the </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:i/><w:iCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">enumeration part</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> scale differently.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="27"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="28" w:name="scaling-law"/><w:p w14:paraId="4DB9DEA4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Scaling law</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6BB2F4A8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Let L = 16 be the number of unit cells, m the number of orbitals per cell, and N_f the particle number. Two separate costs multiply:</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="460CA3EC"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">1. Number of projected configurations.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> With at most one fermion per unit cell and fixed N_f = f·L, the number of allowed configurations is</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="2CA748A4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><m:oMathPara><m:oMathParaPr><m:jc m:val="center"/></m:oMathParaPr><m:oMath><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>#</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>configs</m:t></m:r><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>=</m:t></m:r><m:d><m:dPr><m:begChr m:val="("/><m:endChr m:val=")"/><m:sepChr m:val=""/><m:grow/></m:dPr><m:e><m:f><m:fPr><m:type m:val="noBar"/></m:fPr><m:num><m:r><m:t>L</m:t></m:r></m:num><m:den><m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t>N</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>f</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub></m:den></m:f></m:e></m:d><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>m</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:sSub><m:e><m:r><m:t>N</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sub><m:r><m:t>f</m:t></m:r></m:sub></m:sSub></m:sup></m:sSup><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:limUpp><m:e><m:r><m:rPr><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>→</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:lim><m:r><m:rPr><m:nor/><m:sty m:val="p"/></m:rPr><m:t>half filling</m:t></m:r></m:lim></m:limUpp><m:r><m:t> </m:t></m:r><m:sSup><m:e><m:r><m:t>m</m:t></m:r></m:e><m:sup><m:r><m:t>L</m:t></m:r></m:sup></m:sSup></m:oMath></m:oMathPara></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="7276558E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">(If your BCS state fluctuates in particle number, empty cells are allowed and this becomes (m+1)^L instead.) So the enumeration cost is </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">exponential in L with base m</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — the orbital multiplicity enters as a base, not a prefactor.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="3D267F28"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">2. Cost per configuration.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> Each amplitude is a Pfaffian (paired state) or determinant (Slater) of size N_f × N_f, costing O(N_f³). Crucially, </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">N_f is pinned by the filling (≈ L at half filling), not by m</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — the matrix is 16×16 no matter how many orbitals per cell. So this factor is a constant ~16³ ≈ 4×10³ operations per configuration.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5D31563E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Total: O(m^L · L³) at half filling — i.e., ~10⁴ flops per configuration times m¹⁶ configurations.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="28"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="29" w:name="X6de296ba4a01750b0a643553cc683d4f1de9ef1"/><w:p w14:paraId="5B1A949C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Concrete numbers for a 4×4 cluster (half filling, spinless)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:tbl><w:tblPr><w:tblStyle w:val="Table"/><w:tblW w:type="auto" w:w="0"/><w:jc w:val="left"/><w:tblLook w:firstRow="1" w:lastRow="0" w:firstColumn="0" w:lastColumn="0" w:noHBand="0" w:noVBand="0" w:val="0020"/></w:tblPr><w:tblGrid><w:gridCol w:w="2640"/><w:gridCol w:w="2640"/><w:gridCol w:w="2640"/></w:tblGrid><w:tr><w:trPr><w:tblHeader w:val="on"/></w:trPr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="31BFA6C6"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Orbitals/cell m</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="738BF6C6"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Configs m¹⁶</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="053D232C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Single-core time*</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="5A296522"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">1</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="2A50A1DC"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">1</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="322E8BE4"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">— (trivial)</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="1656D6C2"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">2</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="19D5D576"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">6.5×10⁴</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="4E7A3166"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">seconds</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="41D4DAA2"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">3</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="7AD12F3C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">4.3×10⁷</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="5FA78184"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">~5–10 min (parallel: ~1 min)</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="702B2FCC"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">4</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="0BAB795E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">4.3×10⁹</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="3ED8A536"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">hours single-core; ~tens of minutes on 32 cores</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="10478AE8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">5</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="0EECBE5E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">1.5×10¹¹</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="55FA7512"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">~2 weeks — infeasible</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr><w:tr><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="5F26BC54"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">6</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="379A5F9A"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">2.8×10¹²</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc><w:tc><w:tcPr/><w:p w14:paraId="6B4A60D6"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:jc w:val="left"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">out of reach</w:t></w:r></w:p></w:tc></w:tr></w:tbl><w:p w14:paraId="7C78EE4C"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">*assuming a tight Numba/C loop at ~10 μs per 16×16 Pfaffian.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="36D52E16"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="a0"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">For </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">spinful</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> fermions with m orbitals per cell, one particle per cell can sit in 2m spin-orbitals, so the effective base is d = 2m: single-orbital spinful (d=2) is the easy 65,536-configuration case from before; two-orbital spinful (d=4) is already the “heavy but doable” row.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="29"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="30" w:name="whats-a-reasonable-m-for-44"/><w:p w14:paraId="3934C50E"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">What’s a reasonable m for 4×4?</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="3E78BBC0"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1005"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">m ≤ 3 (spinless) or m = 1 (spinful): exact enumeration is clearly the right method</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — minutes at worst, exact, no approximations.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="0F5B36A8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1005"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">m = 4 (spinless) / m = 2 (spinful): the boundary case.</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> Still feasible with a compiled Pfaffian loop + multiprocessing (the enumeration embarrassingly parallel over configurations), but you’re paying real compute. Worth doing if you need exact numbers.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="2CE6F132"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1005"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">m ≥ 5: brute force enumeration breaks down, but at this system size you don’t need it</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> — Metropolis Monte Carlo over the m¹⁶ configurations with weight |A(c)|² will give the overlap to 4–6 significant digits with ~10⁸ samples, because the configuration space is small and well-behaved. At L=16 the statistical answer is practically the exact one. (For overlaps between two </w:t></w:r><w:r><w:rPr><w:i/><w:iCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">different</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> states, watch for a sign problem if the amplitude ratio changes sign — but on a 16-site cluster the integrand is usually stable enough for VMC to converge well.)</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="30"/><w:bookmarkStart w:id="31" w:name="practical-tips"/><w:p w14:paraId="7AC29064"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="2"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Practical tips</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="6D5A7AB8"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1006"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t>The constant factor matters here: don’t loop in pure Python. A Numba-compiled skew-LDL Pfaffian or a small C extension gets you ~10⁶ configs/s; pure Python is ~100× slower and would demote m=3 to “hours.”</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5D1B06CE"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1006"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Nothing needs to be stored — amplitudes are computed on the fly, so memory is negligible.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="0CAEFE12"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1006"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve">Symmetry reduction buys constant factors only: restricting to a momentum sector divides the sum by ~16, and parity/number sectors by 2–4×. Useful at m=4, irrelevant at m=2.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5E59B894"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Compact"/><w:numPr><w:ilvl w:val="0"/><w:numId w:val="1006"/></w:numPr></w:pPr><w:r><w:t>One caveat: if your filling scales with m rather than being fixed at one particle per cell (e.g., you fill a fraction of the 16m modes), then N_f grows with m and the per-configuration Pfaffian picks up an extra polynomial factor — still secondary compared to the m¹⁶ base, but it softens the “Pfaffian size is constant” statement.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="5AFEF016"><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="FirstParagraph"/></w:pPr><w:r><w:rPr><w:b/><w:bCs/></w:rPr><w:t xml:space="preserve">Bottom line:</w:t></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space="preserve"> exact enumeration comfortably handles up to ~3 orbitals per cell (spinless) on a 4×4 cluster, with 4 as a parallelizable stretch goal; beyond that, Monte Carlo on the same configuration space is the sensible continuation and is effectively exact at this size.</w:t></w:r></w:p><w:p w14:paraId="44B29C76"><w:r><w:pict><v:rect style="width:0;height:1.5pt" o:hralign="center" o:hrstd="t" o:hr="t"/></w:pict></w:r></w:p><w:bookmarkEnd w:id="31"/><w:sectPr w:rsidR="001C7A67" w:rsidSect="005F6CBF"><w:headerReference r:id="rId9" w:type="even"/><w:headerReference r:id="rId11" w:type="default"/><w:footerReference r:id="rId14" w:type="even"/><w:footerReference r:id="rId13" w:type="default"/><w:headerReference r:id="rId10" w:type="first"/><w:footerReference r:id="rId12" w:type="first"/><w:footnotePr><w:numRestart w:val="eachSect"/></w:footnotePr><w:pgSz w:h="15840" w:w="12240"/><w:pgMar w:bottom="1440" w:footer="1020" w:gutter="0" w:header="720" w:left="1800" w:right="1800" w:top="1440"/><w:cols w:space="720"/><w:docGrid w:linePitch="326"/></w:sectPr></w:body></w:document>